      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_cf5ac534",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "silenced_woman_scene",
      "label": "woman_gagged_by_duct_tape",
      "description": "A scene depicting a woman with duct tape over her mouth, symbolizing being silenced or gagged. The woman is the central visual element, posing with one hand on her head and the other on her hip, conveying a sense of being suppressed or restrained. This scene is juxtaposed with text claiming a 'new seat belt design' leads to '50% less accidents,' creating a metaphorical context where the woman's voice or perspective is being ignored in favor of a technical solution.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_dfa730b5",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "misogynous_stereotype",
      "label": "Misogynous Stereotype",
      "description": "The meme relies on the stereotype that women are intellectually or emotionally incapable of contributing meaningfully to technical or safety-related domains, and that their voices are irrelevant or disruptive to progress. This is conveyed through the visual metaphor of a woman being gagged, implying suppression of her input or perspective. The text 'new seat belt design' and '50% less accidents' further reinforces the idea that a technical solution (implied to be male-dominated or 'objective') is being imposed over women's concerns, which are being silenced.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1373e2c1",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "silenced_woman_metaphor",
      "label": "Silenced Woman Metaphor",
      "description": "The image of a woman with duct tape over her mouth is a widely recognized visual trope used to symbolize censorship, suppression, or silencing of women's voices. This metaphor is not visible in the image itself but is understood through cultural knowledge. It is used here to imply that women's perspectives or concerns are being ignored or overridden by a 'new design' that supposedly improves safety, thereby reinforcing harmful stereotypes about women's competence or relevance in technical or safety-related domains.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_2542baf8",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "seat_belt_safety_claim",
      "label": "Seat Belt Safety Claim",
      "description": "The text 'new seat belt design' and '50% less accidents' refers to a real-world concept of automotive safety engineering. The meme uses this claim as a backdrop to juxtapose it with the visual of the silenced woman, implying that the 'new design' is somehow superior or more important than human perspectives, particularly women's. This requires background knowledge of automotive safety and the role of design in accident reduction.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_81970d52",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "gendered_technical_domains_stereotype",
      "label": "Gendered Technical Domains Stereotype",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references the stereotype that technical fields (like automotive engineering or safety design) are male-dominated and that women are not valued or considered competent in these areas. This stereotype is not visible in the image but is necessary to understand the meme's underlying message. The image of the silenced woman is used to reinforce the idea that women's input is being disregarded in favor of a technical solution, which is framed as superior.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b73b37b6",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "silenced_frustration",
      "label": "frustration expressed through being gagged",
      "description": "The woman's pose—hand on head, other hand on hip, with duct tape over her mouth—conveys a strong sense of frustration, helplessness, and suppression. The gagged mouth visually represents her inability to speak or be heard, while the posture suggests exasperation or being overwhelmed. This emotional state is not one of passive compliance but of active resistance or distress at being silenced.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fb0fb3fd",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "The silenced woman is mapped as the 'voice' being suppressed by the 'new seat belt design' claim.",
      "description": "The visual of the gagged woman is mapped onto the textual claim 'new seat belt design' to imply that women's perspectives or voices are being silenced or overridden by a technical solution (the seat belt design) that supposedly improves safety. The woman's gagged state symbolizes the suppression of her input, while the text presents a technical advancement as superior, reinforcing the misogynous stereotype that women are irrelevant or incapable in technical domains. This mapping is established through the juxtaposition of the visual metaphor (silenced woman) with the textual claim (seat belt design), creating an analogy where the woman represents the silenced voice of women in safety engineering.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_d7a40711",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the silenced woman visual metaphor",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the woman with duct tape over her mouth, which symbolizes being silenced or gagged. This projection implies that the user identifies with the experience of having their voice suppressed or ignored, particularly in the context of technical or safety-related domains as suggested by the text. The meme's overall intent, which is misogynous, reinforces this projection by framing the woman's silenced state as representative of women's perspectives being overridden by a 'new seat belt design' claim.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9919a256",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Mysogynous",
      "description": "The meme uses a visual metaphor of a gagged woman to imply that women's voices are being silenced or ignored in the context of a 'new seat belt design' that supposedly reduces accidents by 50%. This reinforces harmful stereotypes that women are intellectually or emotionally incapable of contributing meaningfully to technical or safety-related domains, and that their perspectives are irrelevant or disruptive to progress. The overall intent is misogynous, as it frames women's input as being overridden by a technical solution, thereby diminishing their competence or relevance in such domains.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_4028353f"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
